{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"reasoning","text":"Very interesting findings!\n\nFour key insights on bias and correlation relationships in prediction have been obtained: per-sample bias explains 3-7% of variance and grows with horizon, and base-anchor is strongly correlated with sample-level bias; per-node additive bias outperforms multiplicative bias, and base value is negatively correlated with target/base ratio.\n\nI've clarified the underlying logic of the negative correlation between base value and target/base ratio, confirming that the base forecast has a scaling dynamic range compression issue and tends to underestimate targets.\n\nBase-anchor predicts per-sample bias especially at long horizons with correlation 0.56 at h=11. I will develop a correction method and design the next experiment."}}
